Negative control — the EDITOR's readiness bound (AC-26, design D-10, hazard Z2).

Mutates `whenAvailable`'s expiry back to the pre-conversion
`waitFor(…, { timeout: Infinity })` — the exact wait this story replaces.

Should flip RED — __tests__/client-address.manager-readiness.int.test.ts:
  - "AC-26 settles isReady() within a known limit when the lookup chain never
     returns"                    (races a 20s ceiling; the wait never ends)
  - "AC-26 surfaces the stall as a CATCHABLE rejection carrying a timeout
     status"                     (nothing is ever thrown to catch)

Why this is now a real control. When it was first authored the two AC-26 tests
were ALREADY red — `READINESS_TIMEOUT_MS` was 30s against a 20s ceiling, so the
mutant proved nothing. Both are green as of the bound landing at 15s, so
`Infinity` is the mutation that turns a settling wait back into a hanging one.

Note the SIBLING bound this does NOT cover: the collection half's
`whenListFetched` (`useClientAddresses.actions.ts`) carries its own
`READINESS_TIMEOUT_MS` for AC-4. One control, one mutation — a second control
belongs to that file if AC-4 is to be covered the same way.

